     which means that if a regular expression matches another defined mount point,
     it will override the previously defined threshold.
 
+    A mount can also be given a mapping with ``maximum_usage`` and/or
+    ``minimum_usage`` instead of a plain percentage. ``maximum_usage`` behaves
+    exactly like the plain percentage form above (alert when usage rises at or
+    above it). ``minimum_usage`` alerts when usage unexpectedly drops at or
+    below a threshold, which is useful for catching a mount that is normally
+    busy but silently failed to mount, leaving what looks like the real disk
+    as an empty directory on the root filesystem.
+
+    .. code-block:: yaml
+
+        beacons:
+          diskusage:
+            - /mnt/nfs:
+                minimum_usage: 5%
+                maximum_usage: 90%
+
+    Both forms can be mixed freely, and a mount can appear more than once, so
+    a low-usage alert can be added for a mount that already has a plain
+    high-usage threshold configured without changing that existing entry.
+
+    .. code-block:: yaml
+
+        beacons:
+          diskusage:
+            - /mnt/nfs: 50%
+            - /mnt/nfs:
+                minimum_usage: 5%
+
     """
     whitelist = []
     config = salt.utils.beacons.remove_hidden_options(config, whitelist)
